{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And HaShem appointed a set time, saying: Tomorrow HaShem will do.</b> He should have said 'Tomorrow I will do,' or he should have said 'Tomorrow this thing will come about in the land.' And by way of the plain sense (peshat), it means that the Holy One, blessed be He, appointed a set time for Moses, to say to Israel: Tomorrow HaShem will do this thing in the land." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah —</b> 'And HaShem appointed,' 'HaShem will do' — the intent is 'He and His court.' And He already mentioned 'His court' above, in that He mentioned ״הנה יד ה׳ הויה״ — 'behold, the hand of YHWH shall be' — and that is the designated unique Name; and we find its parallel (Exodus 16:28): ״ויאמר ה׳ אל משה עד אנה מאנתם״ and afterward ״ראו כי ה׳ נתן לכם השבת״ — and it should have said 'I give'; and so there are many such instances."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:12.632105592Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}